%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: stipplekit.ps — density-driven stippling and point-shading primitives
%%Creator: pscat — issue #50
%%EndComments
% @requires: (lib/artkit.ps) run

% A thin convenience layer over lib/artkit.ps's `scatter` (issue #48):
% one operator, `stipple`, that places dots (or any caller-supplied
% point mark) across a region according to a density — constant, or a
% per-point callback for tonal stippling. It deliberately does not
% invent a new placement engine or a competing options format —
% regions, seeding, minimum spacing, attempt/deposit budgets, and size
% variation are all `scatter`'s own vocabulary, unchanged. `stipple`'s
% only real job is deciding what `/Density` means when it is a
% callback, and supplying a sensible default `/Mark` (a filled circle)
% so a caller who just wants stippled dots never has to write one.
%
%   0 0 200 200 screct << /Density 0.02 /Seed 3 >> stipple
%
%   0 0 200 200 screct
%   << /Density { pop 100 div } /MaxDensity 0.02 /MinSpacing 3 >> stipple
%
% `stipple` takes a region exactly as `scatter` does — build one with
% `screct`/`scpath` (a rectangle or the current path; see artkit.ps's
% own docs for the full contract, including `clippath scpath` for
% scattering inside the current clip). Every option below that isn't
% listed is `scatter`'s own and forwarded unchanged: `/Count`,
% `/MinSpacing`, `/Seed`, `/Tries`, `/Budget`, `/Scale`, `/Rotate`,
% `/Mark`, `/Weight`. `scplaced` (artkit.ps) reports how many marks
% actually landed after a `stipple` call, the same as after a raw
% `scatter` one — `stipple` never invents a second readback for a
% number `scatter` already publishes.
%
% `/Density`, constant form: a plain number, forwarded verbatim as
% `scatter`'s own `/Density` (marks per unit area, resolved against
% the region's real area) — uniform stippling, zero new arithmetic.
%
% `/Density`, callback form: a procedure `{x y -> w}`, `w` in [0,1]
% (clamped by `scatter`'s own acceptance roll — see `scodds` in
% artkit.ps — regardless of what the proc actually returns), read as a
% *relative* tone at that point: 1 means "as dense as this stipple
% ever gets here," 0 means "no ink here." This is the same convention
% `lib/hatchkit.ps`'s `/Density` tone callback uses, not a
% newly-invented one. The absolute scale comes from a second, required
% option:
%
% `/MaxDensity num >= 0` — marks per unit area at a relative tone of 1.
% Required only when `/Density` is a callback (ignored, harmless if
% given alongside a constant `/Density` or `/Count`). Internally,
% `stipple` hands `scatter` its own `/Density` as `/MaxDensity` (the
% peak, which is what actually drives `scatter`'s own `Count =
% truncate(MaxDensity * area)`) and the caller's callback as its own
% `/Weight` (which thins acceptance down from that peak, point by
% point). Read the consequence precisely: **the realized total tracks
% `/MaxDensity` times the region's area, not the field's own spatial
% integral** — a field whose average is well below its peak still
% resolves close to the peak-driven count, just reshaped toward the
% high-tone areas by the acceptance roll, exactly the way `scatter`'s
% own `/Weight` has always worked (`scatter_weight_biases_where_marks_
% land` in tests/artkit.rs already pins this for the raw primitive).
% `stipple` does not attempt to make total count track ∫w dA instead —
% that would mean forcing `scatter`'s own `/Tries` down to 1 to turn
% each candidate into a single accept/reject Bernoulli trial rather
% than a retry-until-accepted slot, silently changing what `/Tries`
% means versus every other `scatter`-based library in this codebase.
% A caller who genuinely needs total-count-tracks-the-integral has
% that same option available directly: call `scatter` itself with
% `/Tries 1` and its own `/Weight`.
%
% Since `stipple` hands the caller's own callback straight to `scatter`
% as `/Weight`, it inherits that option's exact contract unchanged: the
% proc MUST consume both operands and leave exactly one number, must
% not touch `sc-`/`sq-`/`si-` names (it runs inside `scatter`'s own
% placement loop, same as a raw `/Weight` would), and an uncallable
% value surfaces as `scatter`'s own `scatter-weight-must-be-a-procedure`
% — `stipple` does not re-validate callability itself, the same
% "don't duplicate what the layer underneath already checks" choice
% `/Count`-vs-callback-`/Density` conflicts rely on below.
%
% `/Count` and a callback `/Density` are mutually exclusive the same
% way `/Count`/`/Density` already are inside `scatter` itself — no
% extra check here; after `stipple` substitutes its own peak/weight
% pair, a caller-supplied `/Count` alongside them simply trips
% `scatter`'s own `scatter-count-and-density-are-mutually-exclusive`.
% `/Weight` and a callback `/Density` ARE checked here
% (`stipple-weight-and-callable-density-are-mutually-exclusive`) —
% unlike `/Count`, `stipple` cannot let this one propagate: it needs
% the `/Weight` slot itself for the callback, and silently overwriting
% a caller's own `/Weight` would be a silent conflict, not a caught
% one. A constant `/Density` (or `/Count`) may still be paired with an
% explicit `/Weight` — that combination isn't `stipple`'s to own, and
% is forwarded straight through.
%
% `/Mark {x y scale angle -> -}` — optional, unlike `scatter`'s own
% (required) `/Mark`. Default: a filled circle, `newpath`ed first (a
% region built via `scpath` leaves the flattened region path behind on
% purpose — see artkit.ps's own docs — so a mark that skipped `newpath`
% would fill that leftover outline on its very first call only, before
% `fill`'s own implicit `newpath` cleared it for every mark after;
% subtle, and exactly the kind of one-shot corruption this file's own
% tests check for directly) of radius `/DotRadius * scale` — `scale`
% comes from `scatter`'s own `/Scale [lo hi]` range, so "dot-size
% variation" is `scatter`'s existing vocabulary, not a new one.
% `/DotRadius num > 0` (default 1.5) is the base radius the default
% mark scales from; unused, harmless, if a caller supplies their own
% `/Mark` — that override is exactly how `stipple` covers genuine
% point-shading with arbitrary marks (crosses, ticks, glyphs), per
% artkit.ps's own one-line index of `scatter`'s shapes: `region
% <</Mark {x y s a ...}>> scatter`.
%
% Reproducibility, safety limits, and Ghostscript compatibility are
% all inherited from `scatter` unchanged (same pinned draw order, same
% `/Seed`/`rrand`/`srand` round-trip, same `/Budget`/`/Tries` bounds,
% same rand generator caveat) — `stipple` adds no randomness of its
% own.
%
% Scratch prefix: `sp-`, distinct from `scatter`'s own `sc-`/`sq-`/
% `si-` and hatchkit's `h-`. A `/Density` callback must not touch
% `sp-` names any more than it may touch `sc-`/`sq-`/`si-` ones —
% `stipple`'s own pre-flight (building the private options copy,
% reading `/DotRadius`) always finishes before `scatter` is ever
% called, so there is no `sp-` state alive during the callback's own
% invocation, but the reserved-prefix discipline is stated here for
% the same reason hatchkit and artkit state theirs: so a future edit
% to this file doesn't introduce one without noticing the contract.

% dict key default spgetdef -> value
% stipplekit's own copy of the wrap-the-default-in-an-array trick —
% see artkit's scgetdef / hatchkit's hkgetdef / paintkit's pkgetdef
% for the same reasoning, each library owning its own copy rather than
% reaching into a sibling file's scratch namespace.
% @internal
/spgetdef {
  /spgdd exch 1 array astore def
  /spgdk exch def
  /spgdt exch def
  spgdt spgdk known { spgdt spgdk get } { spgdd 0 get } ifelse
} def

% v spisnum -> bool
% stipplekit's own copy of artkit's scisnum, for the same reason
% spgetdef is its own copy of scgetdef.
% @internal
/spisnum { type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or } def

% x y scale angle spdefaultmark -
% The default /Mark: a filled circle of radius /DotRadius * scale.
% `newpath` first — see this file's own header for why that isn't
% optional. `spdotradius` is resolved once, before `scatter` runs, by
% `stipple` itself.
% @internal
/spdefaultmark {
  pop
  /spmsc exch def
  /spmy exch def
  /spmx exch def
  newpath
  spmx spmy spdotradius spmsc mul 0 360 arc
  closepath
  fill
} def

% region opts stipple -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Places dots (or a caller-supplied point mark) across a region according to a density — a constant number, or a per-point {x y -> w} tone callback for sparse-to-dense stippling. A thin convenience layer over artkit.ps's scatter; every option not listed here (/Count, /MinSpacing, /Seed, /Tries, /Budget, /Scale, /Rotate, /Mark, /Weight) is scatter's own, forwarded unchanged.
% @example: 0 0 200 200 screct << /Density 0.02 /Seed 3 /MinSpacing 4 >> stipple
% @param: /Density num or {x y -> w} marks per unit area (a number), or a relative [0,1] tone callback paired with /MaxDensity for the absolute scale
% @param: /MaxDensity num >= 0 marks per unit area at a relative tone of 1; required only when /Density is a callback
% @param: /DotRadius num > 0 base radius the default circular /Mark scales from via scatter's own /Scale range (default 1.5)
/stipple {
  % The whole options operand is wrapped before its own type is even
  % checked -- the same auto-execution hazard as every proc-typed
  % option below, just one level up: a caller passing a bare procedure
  % where a dict belongs would otherwise get it bound by `def` and
  % then executed by the very next bare reference meant only to
  % type-check it (Codex review, PR #122, round 2).
  1 array astore /spoptsbox exch def
  /spregion exch def
  spoptsbox 0 get type /dicttype ne { stipple-opts-must-be-a-dict } if
  /spopts spoptsbox 0 get def

  % A private copy -- scatter reads /Density/Weight/Mark from whatever
  % is handed to it, and the callback branch below rewrites both, so
  % the caller's own dict must never be the one mutated.
  /spscopts spopts length 4 add dict def
  spopts spscopts copy pop

  % /DotRadius only feeds the *default* /Mark -- a caller who
  % supplies their own must not be penalized for an unrelated (or
  % malformed) /DotRadius sitting in a shared options dict (Codex
  % review, PR #122, round 2; this file's own header already documents
  % /DotRadius as "unused, harmless" here).
  spscopts /Mark known
  {}
  {
    % Wrapped in a 1-element array before it's ever validated -- a
    % malformed /DotRadius (a caller's procedure, say) would otherwise
    % get bound to a bare name by `def` and then auto-executed by the
    % very next bare reference meant only to type-check it (Codex
    % review, PR #122, round 1 -- the same hazard /Density's own wrap
    % exists to avoid, missed here on the first pass because
    % plain-numeric options looked safe to bare-bind by analogy with
    % hatchkit's own /Spacing/Wobble/Dropout). Only after validation
    % confirms it really is a number is it safe to bind bare --
    % spdefaultmark reads it that way.
    spopts /DotRadius 1.5 spgetdef 1 array astore /spdrbox exch def
    spdrbox 0 get spisnum not { stipple-dotradius-must-be-a-number } if
    spdrbox 0 get 0 le { stipple-dotradius-must-be-positive } if
    /spdotradius spdrbox 0 get def
    spscopts /Mark /spdefaultmark load put
  } ifelse

  spscopts /Density known {
    % Wrapped in a 1-element array, the same trick spgetdef and
    % hatchkit's own hdensityopt use: /Density is legitimately either
    % a number or a procedure here, and a name bound directly to a
    % procedure auto-executes on every bare reference -- fatal here,
    % since nothing is on the operand stack yet for it to consume.
    spscopts /Density get 1 array astore /spdensbox exch def
    spdensbox 0 get spisnum not {
      % Not a number. `stipple` still needs the /Weight slot for
      % whatever this turns out to be (forwarded below regardless),
      % so a caller-supplied /Weight is a conflict either way.
      spscopts /Weight known
        { stipple-weight-and-callable-density-are-mutually-exclusive } if
      % Only route through the /MaxDensity-required contract when the
      % value actually looks callable -- `xcheck` is the same first
      % gate `sccallable` itself applies (safe to call on any object
      % type; it inspects the executable flag without invoking
      % anything), so this never disagrees with what scatter's own
      % real validation would ultimately accept. Something that isn't
      % even executable (a bare string, say) skips straight to being
      % forwarded as scatter's own /Weight below, undisguised, so
      % scatter's real sccallable-backed check is what rejects it --
      % otherwise a non-callable /Density with no /MaxDensity given
      % surfaced the wrong, confusing error (Codex review, PR #122,
      % round 2).
      spdensbox 0 get xcheck {
        spscopts /MaxDensity known not
          { stipple-maxdensity-required-when-density-is-callable } if
        % Same wrap, same reason as /DotRadius above -- a malformed
        % /MaxDensity must fail with a named error, not auto-execute.
        spscopts /MaxDensity get 1 array astore /spmaxdbox exch def
        spmaxdbox 0 get spisnum not { stipple-maxdensity-must-be-a-number } if
        spmaxdbox 0 get 0 lt { stipple-maxdensity-must-be-non-negative } if
        /spmaxd spmaxdbox 0 get def
        spscopts /Density spmaxd put
      } {
        % Not even executable -- scatter's own /Weight validation is
        % what should reject this, not scatter's /Density validation
        % (which a leftover /Density key here would hit first).
        spscopts /Density undef
      } ifelse
      spscopts /Weight spdensbox 0 get put
      spscopts /MaxDensity undef
    } if
  } if

  spregion spscopts scatter
} def
